"""
Log-Rank Test — do two groups survive differently?

WHAT IT DOES: The workhorse of medical trials: treatment vs control, does one live
longer? Survival data is special because of censoring — some patients leave the study
alive (outcome unknown, event=0); you can't just average. The log-rank test compares
the observed vs expected deaths at each moment someone dies, across both groups, and
returns a statistic + p-value. Low p → the groups really differ; high p → no evidence
they do.

WHEN TO USE: You have time-to-event data with censoring and want to know if two groups
have different survival curves. This is the standard nonparametric test — no assumptions
about the shape of the survival curves.

GUARANTEES: Different groups detected (p < 0.10). Same-distribution groups not flagged
as different (p > 0.10). The null uses two INDEPENDENT samples from one distribution
— a real statistical outcome, not a vacuous identity (feeding the same array twice
would force z=0 by construction).

NOT: A robust survival analysis tool. Input-mismatch handling is asymmetric (too-long
events silently ignored, too-short events crash). The var < 1e-14 safety branch has
ZERO test coverage (found by hand-mutation). Invalid event flags (e.g. 5) are silently
treated as censored (fix #12).
"""
